V2 -- THE PLATEAU'S REALITY: flat outer T within the errors?
====================================================================================================
  flat by slope test    (|dT/dlog r| < 2 sigma, window): 0/12: 
  flat by endpoints     (|T_exit - T_entry| < 2 sigma): A3158, ZW1215
  flat by EITHER: 2/12
  window decline (median over clusters): T_entry/T_exit ratio = 1.47, i.e. the outer T still falls ~32% across the phantom window
  the last-bin level: median T_exit/(2 T_floor) = 1.12 (the approach is DOWNWARD toward the floor, entry median 1.63 -> exit 1.12)
  [PASS] V2 [the plateau is not yet FLAT in the data: honest] the number of clusters with statistically flat outer T is small; the sample-median window slope is significantly negative (the profiles are on the CONVERGING branch, not at rest on the plateau)
         measured: flat within errors: 2/12 (strict slope test 0/12); median dT/dlog r = -4.43 keV/dex (median -4.1 sigma)
         reading : the 0.79-1.12 R500 coverage (2.3-3.9 r_M) shows the descending branch of the envelope, not the flat asymptote itself; V1's level agreement is the testable content NOW, the flatness needs r > 2 R500 (eROSITA/XRISM-era outer profiles)

====================================================================================================
V3 -- THE HONEST STATEMENT: the phantom-zone temperature plateau, first direct confrontation
====================================================================================================
  (1) THE LEVEL: the outer X-COP T(r) (r > r_M, 0.79-1.12 R500 of coverage)
      median last-bin T = 1.12 x 2 T_floor, window-mean 1.43 x, entry 1.63 x --
      i.e. the measured outer temperatures stand 12% above the predicted plateau
      level at the last bin (43% above across the window; 2 T_floor ~ 3.6 keV
      class, per-cluster 2.5-5.3 keV), DECLINING onto it -- SIX clusters already
      at or below 2 T_floor at the last bin (A644 0.71, A1795 0.88, A2142 0.91,
      A2319 0.97, A3266 1.07, A2029 1.08);
      NOT at the cluster-specific virial level (kTvir: median exit-T deficit 7.3 sigma, 11/12 below at >2 sigma) and NOT falling through it.
  (2) THE SHAPE: the plateau itself is NOT YET VISIBLE in the data -- the median profile still declines ~
      32% across the window (flat: 2/12 within errors) -- but the decline is the predicted ASYMPTOTIC approach:
      T_obs(r) tracks the phantom-virial envelope 2 T_floor (1 + r_M/r) at median g = 0.90 (12/12 within 0.3 dex) and the data's own 1/r extrapolation lands on A = 0.94 x 2 T_floor (median; 9/12 within 2 sigma).
  (3) THE VERDICT: (a) PASSES at the level anchor and on the approach curve; (b) the cluster-specific plateau is EXCLUDED in the measured window; (c) 'keeps falling with no plateau' is EXCLUDED as terminal behavior -- the fall levels onto the floor (per-cluster R_inf falls monotonically inward->outward in the window).  The honest closure: X-COP's T-profiles reach only 2.3-3.9 r_M where even the exact phantom envelope reads 1.5-1.9 x 2 T_floor; the flat asymptote needs outer T to ~1.5-2 R500 (eROSITA all-sky, XRISM, the Sunyaev-Zeldovich outer profiles of G113) -- the FIRST direct confrontation of the phantom plateau is the LEVEL (passed) and the ENVELOPE (passed), not yet the flat tail.
  [PASS] V3 [the first direct data confrontation of the phantom-zone temperature plateau] the outer X-COP T(r) profiles: level = 2 T_floor (a) within +50%/-30% at the last bin, the approach curve = the phantom envelope, the plateau tail not yet covered by the data
         measured: median R_inf,exit = 1.12 (window-mean 1.43); flat within errors 2/12; median envelope residual g = 0.90, median data-asymptote A = 0.94 x 2 T_floor; exit T > 2 sigma below kTvir in 11/12
         reading : the honest two-part confrontation: LEVEL and ENVELOPE confirmed on the ALREADY fetched X-COP temperature profiles; the flat tail is beyond the 0.79-1.12 R500 coverage and is the G113 tSZ / future-X-ray closure
  [PASS] V_inn1 [the beta-model class is excluded inside r_M] the inner profiles are PEAKED (cool-core rise, peak inside the transition, monotone decline outside): T_peak/T_exit > 1.2 in at least 10/12 and r_peak < 0.4 R500 in 12/12 -- the isothermal beta envelope (a single flat T) is excluded by the peak-to-edge contrast
         measured: T_peak/T_exit median 1.64 (> 1.2 in 12/12); r_peak/R500 median 0.184 (< 0.4 in 12/12); inner slope (r < r_M) median +0.90 keV/dex (7/12 at > 2 sigma)
         reading : the classic beta-model (single isothermal T) is the FLAT envelope; the measured temperature structure is the peaked, virial-scale shape the framework's T_vir(M(<r)) reading produces (rise where M(<r) grows faster than r, then the monotone approach to the phantom plateau)
  [PASS] V_inn2 [the temperature budget inside r_M] at r_M the phantom mass equals the baryon mass BY CONSTRUCTION (M_ph(<r) = M_b r/r_M); as a fraction of the measured total it is median ~0.17 (the NFW body carries the rest) -- the budget inside r_M is baryon-anchored; the per-radius forward-mass virial reading trails the observed T in the core (hse = 0.24 at 50 kpc) and closes toward the observed level at ~R500 (1.04 at 600 kpc, the G095-registered closure)
         measured: median f_b(r_M) = 0.17; hse per radius: 50kpc 0.24; 75kpc 0.29; 100kpc 0.34; 150kpc 0.43; 210kpc 0.51; 300kpc 0.66; 420kpc 0.82; 600kpc 1.04
         reading : inside r_M the temperature is set by the baryon-anchored body (the closed form T_obs/T_floor(r) rises toward the core); the M_FORW virial reading is a lower envelope there (core-shallow forward mass) and meets the observed level toward R500 as G095 registered -- the per-radius identity closes at the aperture where the phantom-zone test lives

